# FEX on Aurora

This is FEX built from Aurora's fork (https://github.com/aurora-silicon/fex),
packaged under Fedora's fex-emu names so that it replaces Fedora's build:
`dnf install fex-emu` installs it. (Fedora's `fex` is an unrelated tool.)

## 4 KiB pages

FEX needs a 4 KiB page size. Aurora kernels use 16 KiB pages but can give a
process tree 4 KiB pages when the kernel is booted with `user4k=on` (the
default on Aurora images; `aurora-userpages` adds it on installed systems).
Start x86 programs inside a 4 KiB tree:

    userpages 4k FEXBash
    userpages 4k FEX ./program

Programs started from a 4 KiB tree inherit it, including x86 programs that
binfmt_misc hands to FEX. Started from an ordinary 16 KiB process,
FEX runs with 16 KiB pages and x86 programs fail. `userpages --status` shows
the current page size. Aurora's Steam launcher starts Steam in a 4 KiB tree.

## Hardware TSO

On kernels that offer the memory-model prctl (PR_SET_MEM_MODEL), FEX switches
the process to the hardware total store order mode and drops its software
TSO emulation. Check with:

    FEXGetConfig --tso-emulation-info

## x86 parity and adjust flags

Apple cores can compute the x86 parity and adjust flags, PF and AF, in
hardware. When the kernel advertises this extension, called APFLG, FEX turns
it on at startup and keeps PF and AF in the host flags instead of computing
them in software. `FEX_HOSTFEATURES=disableapflg` turns it off.

## Root filesystem

FEX needs an x86-64 root filesystem for anything but static programs. The
global config, /usr/share/fex-emu/Config.json, sets RootFS to
/usr/share/fex-emu/RootFS/default.erofs, the image from fex-emu-rootfs-fedora
(dnf installs it with fex-emu unless weak dependencies are turned off).
FEXServer mounts the image with erofsfuse when FEX starts, and runs
erofsfuse with native pages (a 4 KiB page process can't serve a FUSE mount on
Aurora's kernels), so `userpages 4k FEXBash` works without setup. To see the
RootFS in use:

    userpages 4k FEXGetConfig --current-rootfs

If the image is missing, FEX's error names the package to install:

    sudo dnf install fex-emu-rootfs-fedora

## Aurora's x86 Mesa

The stock image carries Fedora's x86 Mesa, which doesn't match Aurora's
kernel and host Mesa. mesa-aurora-fex-guest, built from the same Mesa commit
as the host driver, lays Aurora's x86_64 and i686 Mesa over that image:
aurora-fex-mesa.service mounts the result, the graphics provider, read-only at
/usr/share/guestos/aurora-fex-mesa. fex-emu mounts the image at
/usr/share/guestos/fex-mesa as its lower layer (unless a provider tree is
already there). fex-emu recommends mesa-aurora-fex-guest wherever Aurora's
host Mesa is installed, so dnf adds it on installs and upgrades; on systems
with Fedora's own Mesa it pulls nothing. If the image arrives after the
provider came up without it, fex-emu mounts the provider again over the image.

dnf adds mesa-aurora-fex-guest again at the next fex-emu update if you remove
it. To keep it off, add `exclude_from_weak=mesa-aurora-fex-guest` to the
[main] section of /etc/dnf/dnf.conf. To keep it installed but run x86 programs
on the stock image, disable the provider (Steam's FEX titles then lose it
too):

    sudo systemctl disable --now aurora-fex-mesa.service

or, for one user only, name the stock image in your own config (see below):

    { "Config": { "RootFS": "/usr/share/fex-emu/RootFS/default.erofs" } }

While the provider is mounted, every x86 program FEX runs uses it:
FEXBash, programs that binfmt_misc hands to FEX, and Steam's FEX titles.
After the service mounts the provider, /usr/libexec/fex-emu/aurora-fex-rootfs
bind-mounts it read-only at /run/fex-emu/rootfs and writes
/run/fex-emu/Config.json, a runtime config that FEX reads over the global one:

    {
      "Config": {
        "RootFS": "/run/fex-emu/rootfs"
      }
    }

It writes the file only if the provider has Aurora's Mesa layer, the image's
x86-64 dynamic loader below it, and the layer's x86-64 Vulkan driver in the
same directory. When the service stops, the provider can't be used, or the
file can't be written in full (a full /run), the file goes, and FEX uses the
stock image through erofsfuse as above; the service's journal says why. The file
is gone after a reboot until the service mounts the provider again; the
service runs before user sessions start. The provider is a kernel mount, so
FEXServer starts no FUSE helper for it, and it works from a 4 KiB page tree
as the image does.

FEX runs from the bind, not from the provider's mount point, so the provider
can be unmounted for an upgrade of mesa-aurora-fex-guest while x86 programs
run. Those programs keep the mount they started on until they exit, but, as
with any library upgrade, files they open afterwards can come from the new
build. Programs started afterwards use the new provider. A bind still in use
stays mounted below the new one, and goes at the next change once it is idle.
A FEXServer keeps the RootFS it started with until its last x86 program exits.

Steam's FEX compatibility tool doesn't read the global config. Steam finds
the provider's graphics_provider.json and passes the provider to it, as
before.

## Your own RootFS

Your own config, ~/.config/fex-emu/Config.json (~/.fex-emu/Config.json if
that directory exists), and the FEX_ROOTFS environment variable override the
global RootFS and the provider. A RootFS given as a name rather than a path is
looked up in ~/.local/share/fex-emu/RootFS and /usr/share/fex-emu/RootFS.
`FEXRootFSFetcher` downloads other root filesystems and `FEXConfig` edits
your config. Both are in fex-emu-utils:

    sudo dnf install fex-emu-utils

A RootFS of your own carries its own x86 Mesa. Remove its RootFS entry from
your config to go back to Aurora's.

Fedora Asahi Remix ran FEX in muvm, which set the RootFS only inside its VM.
On Aurora FEX runs on the host and uses the global config.

## binfmt_misc

FEX's own binfmt_misc handlers, FEX-x86 and FEX-x86_64 (in /usr/lib/binfmt.d),
run x86 programs: the kernel starts FEX with the program open and with the
arguments the program was given. FEX also hands the x86 programs it starts,
for example from FEXBash, to the kernel, so they go through the same
handlers. To list the registered handlers:

    grep -Hs interpreter /proc/sys/fs/binfmt_misc/*

The handler registered last wins. Fedora Asahi Remix installs
binfmt-dispatcher, whose x86 handlers register after FEX's. fex-emu turns
them off with files of the same names in /etc/binfmt.d, which register
nothing; their comments say how to turn them back on. binfmt-dispatcher
then runs x86 programs through /usr/libexec/fex-emu/fex-userpages, which
starts FEX in a 4 KiB page tree even from a 16 KiB process and hands FEX
the program as binfmt_misc opened it. Another package's x86 handler, such
as qemu-user-static's, takes x86 programs from FEX in the same way.
